<GuideLayout slug="jpg-to-pdf-under-100kb" faqs={faqs}>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> open <a href="/jpg-to-pdf?limit=100">JPG to PDF</a>, add your photos in order, choose <strong>A4</strong> and the <strong>100KB</strong> limit, then convert. PDFSherpa shares the 100KB budget across your pages, giving larger photos a larger share, and tells you if the result can’t stay readable.</p>

  <h2>Before you start: take a better photo</h2>
  <p>Most of the work happens before you open any tool. A good phone photo of a document compresses three or four times better than a bad one.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Flat and square:</strong> hold the phone directly above the page, not at an angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Even light:</strong> daylight near a window, no flash glare, no shadow of your hand or phone.</li>
    <li><strong>Fill the frame:</strong> get close enough that the paper fills the photo. Background pixels are wasted bytes.</li>
    <li><strong>Plain background:</strong> a dark table behind white paper helps edges stay crisp.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If your phone’s camera app has a document or scan mode, use it. It crops and flattens automatically.</p>

  <h2>Step by step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <a href="/jpg-to-pdf?limit=100">JPG to PDF</a> (the 100KB limit is pre-selected from this link).</li>
    <li>Tap <strong>Select images</strong> and pick your photos. On iPhone, HEIC photos are converted to JPG automatically.</li>
    <li>Drag the thumbnails into the right order. The badge number is the page number.</li>
    <li>Choose <strong>A4</strong> (or Letter in the US) and <strong>Small</strong> margins.</li>
    <li>Press <strong>Convert</strong>. The result shows the final size and a green “Under 100 KB ✓” if it fits.</li>
    <li>Open the PDF, zoom in, and check that names, numbers and signatures are readable.</li>
  </ol>

  <AdSlot variant="inline" slot="guide-article" />

  <h2>If text becomes hard to read</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reduce the page count:</strong> does the form really need both sides of your ID? Many accept the front only.</li>
    <li><strong>Crop tighter:</strong> use <a href="/resize-image">Resize Image</a> with “Crop to fill” to trim edges first.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask for more room:</strong> if there are separate upload fields, convert each document to its own PDF.</li>
    <li><strong>Try 200KB</strong> if the portal allows “up to 200KB”. Many people pick 100KB out of habit.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Common form requirements, and the catch</h2>
  <p>
    Application portals often ask for “PDF, maximum 100KB” or “between 50KB and 100KB”. If there’s a minimum size too, and your PDF is too
    small, convert again with the 200KB limit or no limit. Requirements vary between portals and change over time, so always read the
    instructions on the official site rather than relying on a number you saw elsewhere.
  </p>

  <h2>Private by default</h2>
  <p>
    Photos of mark sheets, ID cards and certificates are exactly the kind of files that shouldn’t sit on someone else’s server. PDFSherpa converts
    them inside your browser. Nothing is uploaded, and it works offline after your first visit.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
